{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to explain rational preference without induction, yet it explicitly admits this concept is 'formally incoherent' per the Miller-Tichý refutation. By conceding that the metric for 'closeness to truth' fails logically, the argument collapses into the very 'mere habit' or arbitrary proceduralism it sought to avoid. If the normative link to truth is severed because the measure of that link is incoherent, the preference for better-corroborated theories cannot be defended as 'rational' on Popper's own terms; it becomes an unjustified psychological disposition, thereby failing to answer how one avoids smuggling in induction while claiming a non-inductive rational basis for action.","premise":"A rational preference requires a coherent normative metric to distinguish itself from mere habit or arbitrary choice.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude is logically incoherent, what specific non-inductive criterion does Popper offer to justify preferring Theory A over Theory B that isn't simply a restatement of past survival (which risks circularity) or a disguised probability claim?","target":"f426dfdc1087dbf268d19c0c19f9d6f47e3f0b4cb083c0ac915f5d1afb2394a2"}]}